.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS CPUID
.IP
.EX
#define GET_CPUID(LEAF, AX, BX, CX, DX)
#define get_cpuid(leaf, ax, bx, cx, dx)
.EE
.PP
Executes the \f[CR]CPUID\f[R] instruction with the specified
\f[B]leaf\f[R] value and stores the resulting register outputs in the
provided variables.
.PP
The macro places the leaf value into \f[CR]EAX\f[R], runs the
\f[CR]CPUID\f[R] instruction, and then writes the resulting values of
\f[CR]EAX\f[R], \f[CR]EBX\f[R], \f[CR]ECX\f[R], and \f[CR]EDX\f[R] into
the given variables.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
unsigned int eax, ebx, ecx, edx;

get_cpuid(0, eax, ebx, ecx, edx);
.EE
.PP
After execution:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]eax\f[R] contains the \f[B]maximum supported CPUID leaf\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ebx\f[R], \f[CR]edx\f[R], and \f[CR]ecx\f[R] together contain the
\f[B]CPU vendor string\f[R].
.PP
The \f[B]leaf\f[R] selects which processor information block is
returned.
.PP
Common examples:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Leaf
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]0x0\f[R]
T}@T{
Maximum supported leaf and vendor ID
T}
T{
\f[CR]0x1\f[R]
T}@T{
Processor version and feature flags
T}
T{
\f[CR]0x7\f[R]
T}@T{
Structured extended feature flags
T}
T{
\f[CR]0x80000000\f[R]
T}@T{
Maximum supported extended leaf
T}
.TE
.PP
Different leaves return different data layouts in the output registers.
.PP
Example extracting the vendor string:
.IP
.EX
unsigned int    eax, ebx, ecx, edx;
char            vendor[13];

get_cpuid(0, eax, ebx, ecx, edx);

((unsigned int*)vendor)[0] = ebx;
((unsigned int*)vendor)[1] = edx;
((unsigned int*)vendor)[2] = ecx;

vendor[12] = 0;

printf(\(dq[%s]\(rsn\(dq, vendor); \f[I]// [GenuineIntel] (Aka: CPUID_VENDOR_INTEL)\f[R]
.EE
